## From neurons to layers

A neural network is built from small units called neurons. A **neuron** computes a weighted sum of its inputs. Each input is multiplied by its weight (a number that says how much that input matters), the products are added, and a bias (an extra number added to the total) is added as well. The result then goes through a non-linear activation function, which reshapes the number before the neuron passes it on.

::image{id="img_1"}

A layer stacks many neurons that read the same input vector (the list of input numbers). Each neuron has its own weights, so one layer produces one output value per neuron. The activation is what makes a stack of layers more expressive than a single linear transformation. Without it, stacking layers would add nothing, because a chain of linear steps is still just one linear step.

::image{id="img_2"}

In a fully connected layer every neuron reads every output of the previous layer. The number of weights therefore grows as the product of the two layer sizes, which is why wider layers cost more to train.

::quiz{ref="q1"}

## Activation functions

The activation decides how strong the neuron's output is. A rectified linear unit (ReLU) passes positive values unchanged and turns negative values into zero. Other activations, such as the sigmoid, squeeze the output into a bounded range.

::widget{ref="w1"}

